  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Piranesi's development as an etcher may be studied most comprehensively in the ' Views of Rome ', extending as they do throughout his whole life.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Arthur M. Hind",
          "title": "Giovanni Battista Piranesi: A Critical Study",
          "publisher": "The Cotswold Gallery (Internet Archive scan)",
          "year": "1922",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/giovannibattist00hinduoft",
          "readAt": "https://archive.org/stream/giovannibattist00hinduoft/giovannibattist00hinduoft_djvu.txt",
          "accessed": "2026-11-08",
          "tier": 2,
          "page": "14"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "It is well to have the Piranesi view of the Pantheon, now that the belfries have been taken away and the abortive secondary [pediment] has also disappeared, and the building is put, as nearly as modern archaeologists can do it, back into its original form.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Russell Sturgis",
          "title": "The Etchings of Piranesi",
          "publisher": "Frederick Keppel & Co. (Internet Archive scan)",
          "year": "1900",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/etchingsofpirane00stur",
          "readAt": "https://archive.org/stream/etchingsofpirane00stur/etchingsofpirane00stur_djvu.txt",
          "accessed": "2026-11-08",
          "tier": 2
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Throughout his lifetime, Piranesi created numerous prints depicting the Eternal City; these were widely collected by gentlemen on the Grand Tour.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Giovanni Battista Piranesi",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Battista_Piranesi",
          "accessed": "2026-11-08",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "the ' Views of Rome ', the inevitable purchase of successive tourists in Rome, are for the most part mere wrecks of their former selves.",
        "lead": "Hind on later impressions pulled from the worn plates:",
        "source": {
          "author": "Arthur M. Hind",
          "title": "Giovanni Battista Piranesi: A Critical Study",
          "publisher": "The Cotswold Gallery (Internet Archive scan)",
          "year": "1922",
